[size=6][b]Camera Controls — Cinematic Camera Timeline for StarRupture[/b][/size]

A keyframe-based camera timeline that lets you create cinematic camera shots in StarRupture.

Fly a free camera around your base, drop keyframes wherever you want the shot to pass through, then scrub, edit and play the entire camera move back — like a video editing timeline, except the timeline drives the game's actual camera.

[quote][b][color=#ea9999]Warning: This plugin is not your normal plugin, it changes StarRupture into a timeline based video editor, its rather advanced.  Please ensure you have a save backed up before using it on one.  If you want to see a demo of what this plugin can do, check the Videos section.[/color][/b][/quote]

[b]Client-side only[/b]

[line]

[size=5][b]Support[/b][/size]

[b]Found a bug?[/b] Report it on [url=https://github.com/AlienXAXS/StarRupture-Plugin-CameraControls/issues]GitHub[/url] and attach your [b]ModLoader.log[/b] (in [b]Binaries\Win64\ModLoader\Logs\[/b]).

[quote][b]If you have a question or want to discuss the mod, please join our [url=https://discord.gg/G5a4GYsvrz]Discord[/url].[/b][/quote]

[line]

[size=5][b]What It Does[/b][/size]

Camera Controls gives you a complete in-game camera editor for creating smooth, controlled camera movements and cinematic shots.

[list]
[*][b]Free-fly camera[/b] — detach the camera from your character and freely fly around using WASD + QE, mouse look, boost, crawl, roll and live FOV controls.
[/*]
[*][b]Keyframes[/b] — record the camera's position, rotation and FOV at the press of a key.
[/*]
[*][b]Timeline editor[/b] — edit camera moves using a draggable playhead, trimming, ripple editing, zoom and pan.
[/*]
[*][b]Per-keyframe controls[/b] — adjust speed, smoothness, ease in/out, FOV, focus, fades and optional look-at targets.
[/*]
[*][b]Animated depth of field[/b] — give every keyframe its own focus distance and aperture to create focus pulls between shots.
[/*]
[*][b]Rupture cues[/b] — place timeline markers that can start, pause, resume, cancel and manipulate Rupture events during playback.
[/*]
[*][b]Live scrubbing[/b] — drag the playhead and the camera follows the path immediately.
[/*]
[*][b]3D camera path[/b] — keyframes are displayed in the world as a spline with camera frustums and selectable handles.
[/*]
[*][b]Quick shot selection[/b] — double-click a keyframe to fly the camera directly to that exact pose.
[/*]
[*][b]Multi-selection[/b] — Ctrl+click multiple keyframes and cues, then move or delete them as a group.
[/*]
[*][b]Playback mode[/b] — run a clean take with a countdown while the editor and game HUD are out of the way.
[/*]
[*][b]Projects saved to disk[/b] — projects use editable JSON files stored under [i]Plugins\CameraControls\Projects[/i].
[/*]
[*][b]Viewport integration[/b] — the game view is fitted into the available space around the editor panels rather than simply covering the shot.
[/*]
[/list]
[line]

[size=5][b]Getting Started[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Load a save.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]F7[/b] to open the editor.
[/*]
[*]Fly the camera to where your shot should begin.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]K[/b] to create the first keyframe.
[/*]
[*]Fly to the next position and press [b]K[/b] again.
[/*]
[*]Continue adding keyframes until your camera move is complete.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]Space[/b] to preview the timeline.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]F8[/b] to run the shot in playback mode.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]F7[/b] again to leave the editor and restore your character.
[/*]
[/list]
The first time the editor is opened, it displays a short warning explaining that your character is temporarily removed from the active world while editing and that timeline cues can affect the save you are currently playing.

The keybind cheat-sheet remains visible in the editor and shows your actual configured bindings.

[b]Tip:[/b] Right-click the timeline track to quickly add a keyframe or world cue at the position you clicked.

[line]

[size=5][b]Default Keybinds[/b][/size]

All keybinds can be changed through the ModLoader plugin configuration or [i]CameraControls.ini[/i].

[list]
[*][b]F7[/b] — Enter / leave the editor
[/*]
[*][b]F8[/b] — Start / stop playback mode
[/*]
[*][b]F9[/b] — Hide / show editor windows
[/*]
[*][b]Esc[/b] — Stop a take and return to the editor
[/*]
[*][b]W A S D[/b] — Fly forward / left / back / right
[/*]
[*][b]E / Q[/b] — Fly up / down
[/*]
[*][b]Left Shift / Left Ctrl[/b] — Fly faster / slower
[/*]
[*][b]Right Mouse[/b] — Look around
[/*]
[*][b]Mouse Wheel[/b] — Adjust fly speed while looking
[/*]
[*][b]Z / C[/b] — Roll left / right
[/*]
[*][b]X[/b] — Level the camera
[/*]
[*][b]R / F[/b] — Zoom in / out
[/*]
[*][b]K[/b] — Add a keyframe
[/*]
[*][b]I[/b] — Insert a keyframe after the selected keyframe
[/*]
[*][b]U[/b] — Re-record the selected keyframe
[/*]
[*][b]G[/b] — Fly to the selected keyframe
[/*]
[*][b]Delete[/b] — Delete the selected keyframe
[/*]
[*][b], / .[/b] — Select previous / next keyframe
[/*]
[*][b]Space[/b] — Preview play / pause
[/*]
[/list]
[line]

[size=5][b]Timeline Editing[/b][/size]

Each camera segment has its own duration and speed. Changing the speed of a segment automatically changes its effective duration and shifts everything after it accordingly.

[b]Smoothness[/b] controls how the camera transitions between straight-line movement and curved spline movement.

[b]Ease[/b] controls the camera's velocity through each segment, with the outgoing keyframe controlling the beginning of the segment and the incoming keyframe controlling the end.

[b]Fades[/b] can be applied around keyframes to fade the shot into or out of a chosen colour.

[list]
[*]Click a keyframe to select it.
[/*]
[*]Drag a keyframe to retime it.
[/*]
[*]Double-click a keyframe to fly the camera to it.
[/*]
[*]Click a clip to select the segment.
[/*]
[*]Use the mouse wheel to zoom the timeline.
[/*]
[*]Middle-drag to pan the timeline.
[/*]
[*]Use Ctrl+click to select multiple keyframes and cues.
[/*]
[/list]
When multiple items are selected, dragging one moves the entire selection while preserving the spacing between them.

[line]

[size=5][b]Rupture Cues [Experimental][/b][/size]

Alongside camera keyframes, the timeline supports [b]func frames[/b] — cues that control the world's Rupture events during a take.

Right-click the timeline to add a cue.

[list]
[*][b]Start rupture[/b] — starts a Heat or Cold Rupture.
[/*]
[*][b]Set rupture phase[/b] — restarts the active Rupture at a chosen phase.
[/*]
[*][b]Set rupture progress[/b] — jumps to a specific point within the current phase.
[/*]
[*][b]Ramp rupture progress[/b] — smoothly moves Rupture progress between two percentages over a chosen duration.
[/*]
[*][b]Cancel rupture[/b] — immediately ends the active Rupture.
[/*]
[*][b]Pause / Resume rupture[/b] — pauses or resumes the active Rupture.
[/*]
[/list]
[b]Rupture progress can be animated.[/b]

Enable [i]Ramp over time[/i] on a Set rupture progress cue, choose a starting percentage, ending percentage and duration, and the Rupture will smoothly progress between those values while the playhead moves through the ramp.

This makes it possible to synchronise a changing Rupture state with a camera move.

[b]Important:[/b] Rupture cues affect the save you are currently playing. They do not have an undo function.

Cues can be enabled or disabled individually, and [i]Trigger now[/i] can be used to activate a cue directly from its inspector.

Rupture cues require you to be running the world yourself — either in single-player or as the host. Connected clients do not have authority over the server's Rupture state.

[line]

[size=5][b]Depth of Field[/b][/size]

Depth of field can be enabled for individual shots.

Each keyframe can store its own:

[list]
[*]Focus distance
[/*]
[*]Aperture
[/*]
[*]Focus target
[/*]
[/list]
This allows you to create cinematic focus pulls simply by setting different focus distances on different keyframes.

Depth of field is [b]off by default[/b], so the game keeps its normal camera behaviour until you enable it.

[line]

[size=5][b]Camera & Player Safety[/b][/size]

When the camera is detached, Camera Controls temporarily moves your character out of harm's way while you film.

Your character's position, movement mode and gravity settings are saved before entering the editor.

The character is frozen and moved far below the terrain into a protected habitat shelter while the camera is being controlled.

When you leave the editor, your character is returned to their original position and their previous movement state is restored.

The camera itself does not follow the character. The game's world streaming follows the active camera view, allowing you to film distant areas without dragging your character through the shot.

The plugin also protects the player from several causes of instant death while filming, including world bounds, damage and certain hazard/kill triggers.

These protections are restored when you leave the editor.

[line]

[size=5][b]Input & Controls[/b][/size]

By default, the editor uses exclusive input capture while composing.

This prevents normal game input from interfering with camera editing — for example, accidentally firing a weapon or opening the pause menu while working on a shot.

If you want the game to continue receiving input while editing, enable:

[i][Editor] PassthroughInput = true[/i]

This allows the game and editor to share input where appropriate.

[b]Playback mode always uses exclusive input capture.[/b]

The plugin also restores the character's normal input mappings when leaving the editor.

[line]

[size=5][b]Layout[/b][/size]

The editor automatically fits the game's 3D view into the space left between the timeline and inspector panels.

This allows you to keep the camera view visible while working with the editor.

[b]Note:[/b] The preview is cropped rather than scaled, so playback can show more of the game view than the editor preview.

If exact framing is important, disable the viewport fitting behaviour.

[line]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]StarRupture
[/*]
[*]StarRupture ModLoader with [b]v1.23.0 or later[/b]
[/*]
[/list]
The plugin requires ModLoader v1.23.0 or later for its camera path rendering, cooperative input handling and timeline mouse-wheel controls. After a game update that moves something the plugin reads, only the camera tool that depends on it switches off; the rest keeps working.

[b]This is a client-only plugin.[/b] There is no dedicated-server component.

[line]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the latest release from the [b]Files[/b] tab.
[/*]
[*]Open the downloaded ZIP.
[/*]
[*]Copy the included [i]CameraControls.dll[/i] into the game's [i]Modloader\[/i][i]Plugins[/i] directory next to [i]dwmapi.dll[/i].
[/*]
[*]Launch StarRupture.
[/*]
[*]Press [b]F7[/b] to open the Camera Controls editor.
[/*]
[/list]
On first run, [i]CameraControls.ini[/i] is generated in:

[i]<game_dir>\Plugins\config[/i]

[line]

[size=5][b]Troubleshooting[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]The editor does not open[/b] — make sure the plugin is installed correctly and that your ModLoader is v1.23.0 or later (interface v75).
[/*]
[*][b]The camera controls do not work[/b] — check your configured keybinds and make sure the plugin is receiving input.
[/*]
[*][b]The character does not respond after leaving the editor[/b] — close and reopen the editor to retry the input restoration.
[/*]
[*][b]Rupture cue does nothing[/b] — you must be playing single-player or hosting the world. Connected clients cannot control the server's Rupture state.
[/*]
[*][b]The preview framing looks different from playback[/b] — the editor viewport is cropped rather than scaled. Disable viewport fitting if exact framing is required.
[/*]
[*][b]Need to diagnose input issues[/b] — press [b]Home[/b] to write a control-state snapshot to the log.
[/*]
[/list]

